1. The Database Reality: How Parallel Branches Exist

Because they never look back at main, creating a parallel world in your Postgres event log is simple. You use a Log Inheritance Pointer.
When a player forks the timeline in the year 2000:
1. You create branch_world_2 in your database.
2. Its divergence_event_id points to the last valid event in the year 2000.
3. To play the game on branch_world_2: Your engine queries all events from main up to that year 2000 pointer, and then appends all new events exclusively to branch_world_2.
main continues to grow independently. branch_world_2 grows independently. They are two parallel lines that never intersect again.

2. Can the Player Ever Return to Main?

Under this strict parallel architecture, there are only two ways a player can logically "return" to the main timeline:
• The TARDIS Method (Active Travel): The player uses a literal piece of gameplay—a multiverse portal, a specific spell, or a dimension-hopping device—to explicitly jump tracks. Mechanically, your engine just changes their active session pointer from branch_world_2 back to main.
• The "Undo" Method (Chronal Collapse): The player decides they ruined their parallel world and wants to erase it. Mechanically, you delete branch_world_2 and its events from Postgres, snapping the player's active session back to main as if the split never happened.
If they return via Active Travel, they are a traveler entering a world that moved on without them. Other players on main will see them materialize out of thin air, clutching gear or memories from a timeline that technically doesn't exist in main history.

3. The Multiplayer Problem: Solitude in the Multiverse

If every major historical disruption forks a player into their own private timeline, your multiplayer game quickly devolves into a collection of single-player games.
To keep players together without breaking your parallel world engine, you can implement three design patterns:

Pattern A: "Party Branching" (The Cohort System)

Branches shouldn't belong to a single player; they should belong to a Party.
• If Player A and Player B are in a group together and Player A assassinates the Baron in the past, the engine forks both players onto branch_world_2 simultaneously. They travel to the parallel future together, sharing the same alternate sandbox, while the rest of the server stays on main.

Pattern B: The "Stash & Tether" Rule (Soft Constraints)

You can dramatically reduce accidental branching by introducing in-game mechanics that prevent timeline splits unless the players explicitly want to create a new world.
• Fixed History: The LLM is instructed that important historical figures are heavily guarded or "temporally shielded."
• If a player tries to kill the Baron, the engine uses the LLM to cleanly defend history: "As you draw your blade, a localized time-storm deflects your strike, forcing you back into the portal." This keeps players tethered to main for standard play, saving true branching for major, deliberate campaign milestones.

Pattern C: World Merging Hubs (The Anchor Dimension)

You can designate specific zones in your RPG—like a "Tavern at the End of Time" or a central "Planar City"—that exist outside of the timeline.
• This specific area is tied to a static database ID that ignores branching. No matter which parallel universe a player is currently stuck in, when they enter this hub, they can see, chat, and trade with players from main or any other branch.

