Yes, you can absolutely merge a parallel branch (let's call it prime) back into main after a hard fork. However, because you previously cut ties with the main history, a standard database or text-file merge is impossible.
Instead, you have to perform what is known as a Semantic Override or a State Injection.
Instead of merging the history (the events), you take the end-state results of the prime branch and forcefully inject them into the main timeline as a brand-new, monumental event.
Here are the three cleanest architectural ways to make this happen in your LLM RPG engine:

Method 1: The "Dimensional Convergence" (The Cleanest RPG Fit)

Rather than trying to rewrite the past of the main branch—which would corrupt the experience of any other players on it—you treat the merge as a literal narrative event on main.
1. The DB Action: You leave all the historical rows of main completely alone. You find the current tip (the most recent event) of main.
2. The Injection: You insert a special system event row directly onto main that summarizes the state of the player and their achievements from the prime branch.
3. The Narrative Merge: You pass a prompt to the LLM to handle the collision of realities.
