A combination of Method 1 (Narrative Convergence) and Method 3 (Patchwork Merge) is the ultimate way to handle this. The player physically drops back into main (Method 1), but their items, companions, and status effects are programmatically pulled through the rift with them (Method 3).
Because you are forcefully injecting entities generated in an isolated parallel world into the original world, Method 3 is a minefield for deterministic and semantic conflicts.
Here is a breakdown of the specific conflicts this injection will cause and how your Postgres event-sourced engine can resolve them.

1. The Three Core Conflicts of Patchwork Merging

When you pull entities across timelines, your engine will encounter three distinct types of collisions:
[Prime Entity Payload] ────► [Conflict Pipeline] ────► [Main Timeline Event Log]
                                    │
         ┌──────────────────────────┼──────────────────────────┐
         ▼                          ▼                          ▼
 1. ID Collisions           2. Uniqueness Violations   3. Causal Paradoxes
(Deterministic SQL)          (Global Constraints)       (LLM / Semantic)

Conflict A: ID Collisions (The Deterministic Conflict)

• The Scenario: In the prime branch, the engine generated a new weapon and assigned it item_uuid: 101. Coincidentally, while the player was away, the main branch also generated a totally different item and assigned it item_uuid: 101.
• The Fix (Database Layer): This is solved strictly in your Postgres code. Before appending the creation event to main, the engine runs a check: SELECT 1 FROM events WHERE entity_uuid = 101 AND branch_id = 'main'. If it exists, the engine generates a brand-new UUID for the incoming item and updates all references in the incoming player's inventory log before committing.

Conflict B: Uniqueness Violations (The Lore Conflict)

• The Scenario: In the prime branch, the player killed a dragon and forged The One and Only Dragon-Slayer Shield. However, back on main, another player already killed that same dragon and forged the exact same unique shield.
• The Paradox: If you merge the item, two copies of a globally unique, lore-breaking item now exist simultaneously on main.
• The Fix (Rule Layer): Your entities should have a template_id or a uniqueness_flag. If a player tries to bring a globally unique item into main where it already exists, the engine forces a degradation event.
• The SQL Solution: It appends the item to main, but mutates its properties.
• The Narrative Output: "As you cross the rift back to Main, the timeline rejects the paradox of two identical shields. Your shield cracks, its cosmic energy bleeding away, leaving you with a battered, non-magical Steel Shield."

Conflict C: Causal Paradoxes (The Semantic Conflict)

This is the most complex conflict because it involves NPCs and relationships.
• The Scenario: In the prime branch, the player befriended a Goblin named Grip. Grip became their loyal companion. The player merges back into main and brings Grip with them.
• The Paradox: On the main timeline, Grip is currently a hostile bandit captain ruling a dungeon. Grip cannot be both a loyal companion standing next to the player in the capital city AND a hostile bandit boss sitting in a dungeon miles away.
• The Fix (The Twin/Clone Engine): You cannot merge the existing main Grip row. Instead, you must treat the incoming Grip as a Chronal Echo (a new entity altogether).

2. How the Postgres + LLM Resolution Pipeline Handles It

To safely execute a Patchwork Merge, your engine should follow a strict automated script when a player requests to "return to main":
1. The Inventory Scan: Query the final state of the player on prime. Identify every entity ID currently held by or bonded to the player.
2. The Main-Check: For every entity, check if it already exists on main.
	• If it does not exist on main: Append a SPAWN_ENTITY event directly onto the tip of main with a system note: origin: branch_prime.
	• If it does exist on main (like Grip the Goblin): Clone the entity, assign it a brand-new UUID, and append a SPAWN_ECHO_ENTITY event to main.
3. The LLM Synthesizer Step: Pass the conflict payload to the LLM to write the final entry that blends Method 1 and Method 3 together.
